comments[6]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-13T20:21:35.357Z","harness:claude-code","Investigation note (2026-11-13), premise check for the add-without-run half: `pm test <id> --add` has never executed a command. On v2026.11.4 and on main, `runTest` only calls `executeSelectedLinkedTests` when `options.run === true`, and `--add` persists the entry inside `resolveLinkedTestItem` with `run_results: []`. A sandbox repro on the current build confirms it: `--add command=\"node t.js\" --json` returned `run_results: []`. The misleading part was the help text. `--add` and `--add-json` read \"Add verified isolated test\", so the fleet ran `--add ... --run` to \"verify\", which re-runs every selected suite. A metadata-only fix already has a no-run path: `--update-index <n> --set-json '{...}'`.\n\nResolution of that half: the help now reads \"Link isolated test; runs only with --run\" for both flags, and docs/TESTING.md and docs/COMMANDS.md state that `--add` never executes and point to `--set-json` for metadata fixes. No `verified` provenance flag was added. Every `--add` is unverified, so the marker would be constant, and provenance is hashed into the trust fingerprint, so flipping it after a run would invalidate clone-local acknowledgements.\n\nAC refinement (third clause): \"an explicit add-without-run option records verified false\" becomes \"--add records a command without running it, help and docs say so, and pm test --run still executes it\". The setup-phase clauses (1, 2 and 4) are implemented as specified.\n"
  "2026-11-13T20:23:37.833Z","harness:claude-code","Evidence, mixed-host check (2026-11-13). The published @unbrained/pm-cli@2026.11.13 was installed into an isolated npm prefix. npx is not valid for this check, because `npm install -g .` links the working tree under the same name@version, so npx runs the new build. Against a sandbox tracker whose item carries `phase: setup` at `pm_format_version: 3`:\n- `get`, `update --priority 3` and `test --run` all refuse with \"Stored item uses format version 3; this runtime supports up to 2. Upgrade pm before reading or changing\". The item file is byte-identical afterwards.\n- `list` still lists the item, because its light metadata read does not parse linked tests.\nSo an older host refuses the setup entry rather than silently dropping it.\n\nDogfooding decision: this PR adds no setup entry to this repository's own tracker items. Any pinned or bundled older SDK reading the tracker would refuse such an item until it upgrades; package consumers such as pm-changelog are one example. Adopt setup entries here only after the release that ships format 3 is the pinned floor.\n"
  "2026-11-13T21:36:32.074Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 1 on PR #1723 (Codex gpt-6.1-sol xhigh plus Greptile, 2026-11-13). Every finding was valid and is fixed in the follow-up commit.\n1. (major, Codex and Greptile) In test-all, a setup refused before dispatch (an assignment-only command such as X=1, removed by resolveTestAllCommandRefusal) never reached the gate, so later commands ran. Fix: runTestAllItem drives a setup gate over its setup-first entries. A pre-dispatch setup refusal is recorded (phase setup, own category), and every later entry gets a blocked skip row without being dispatched.\n2. (major, Codex) Cross-item regular-test dedupe ignored the item's setup. Two items with the same assertion command and different setups in isolated sandboxes ran it once. Fix: buildTestAllDedupeKey appends the item's setup signature (its setup keys), so a regular test is deduplicated only between items whose setups match.\n3. (major, Greptile) A regular command reserved its dedupe key before its setup ran. When the first item's setup failed, a later item's identical command was skipped as a duplicate although it never executed. Fix: when any setup row of an item did not pass, the keys that item reserved are released.\n4. (major, Codex and Greptile) A setup that never executed (a path-only entry promoted with --set-json, or a runtime safety skip) skipped every dependent behind ok: true. Fix: the gate turns a non-refused, non-passing setup into failed with setup_failed, so the run fails.\n5. (major, Codex) The exported SDK runLinkedTests did not order setup entries itself. Fix: it runs orderLinkedTestsSetupFirst internally and returns results in run order. That is idempotent for pm test and test-all, which already pass setup-first lists.\n6. (minor, Codex) Identical setups in test-all inherited the widest timeout across items. Fix: buildEffectiveTimeoutByKey skips setup entries, and keys regular tests by setup signature, so each setup runs with its own timeout.\nNegative control: with the committed batch.ts restored, the three new test-all cases fail (refused-setup blocking, setup-aware dedupe with key release, timeout keying).\n"
  "2026-11-13T22:25:29.082Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 2 on PR #1723 (Codex gpt-6.1-sol xhigh on 9182dbdf1, 2026-11-13). Codex verified the round-1 fixes and found two more test-all defects, both valid and fixed.\n1. (major) The setup signature reused buildLinkedTestKey, which collapses whitespace inside quoted strings. Two isolated items whose setups wrote 'a  b' and 'a b' shared a signature, so the second item's failing test was skipped as a duplicate. Fix: resolveSetupSignature is now a stableStringify of each setup's executable definition, which keeps command bytes verbatim. Provenance, editor stamps, note and timeout are left out: they do not change what a setup produces, and a setup that times out releases its keys.\n2. (minor) A refusal planned for a later entry (an assignment-only X=1) was reported as config_refusal even when an earlier setup had failed at run time, so it counted as a second failure. Fix: result assembly replays the executed setup outcomes through a run-time gate. A planned refusal after a failed setup becomes the blocked skip, and entries the sandbox already skipped get the same blocked row. The gate now records an already-typed setup_failed row unchanged, so it is idempotent on replay.\nSpecs: the whitespace-signature keys in the timeout-keying case, plus a new runtime-failure-then-planned-refusal case in tests/integration/linked-tests/linked-test-setup-phase.integration.spec.ts.\n"
  "2026-11-13T22:33:50.541Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 3 on PR #1723 (CodeRabbit on 9182dbdf1, 2026-11-13). Two valid findings, both fixed.\n1. (major, pm-gh1671) A setup command carrying --test-name-pattern (or a -t filter on a direct runner) still triggered the implicit name-filter receipt check in buildLinkedTestPassedOrAssertionResult, so a successful build became setup_failed and blocked the item. Fix: setup entries are exempt from that implicit check, and explicit assert_* metadata still applies. Spec: the filtered-setup case, with a negative control that the same command as a regular test still fails empty_run.\n2. (minor, pm-v1mp64) Lean output dropped a passing untrusted row's streams once a single-row run hoisted its context, which discarded the evidence the untrusted override keeps. Fix: isUntrustedRow reads both the full and the reduced context, and lean removal skips untrusted rows. Spec: the lean untrusted case in linked-test-projection.spec.ts.\n"
  "2026-11-13T22:54:51.259Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 4 on PR #1723 (Codex on fe6215b95, 2026-11-13). One minor receipt finding, fixed. Scenario: a setup fails at run time, a later setup was refused at planning (assignment-only), and a regular entry was skipped behind that refusal. The refused setup was correctly reported as skipped, but the regular entry's planning-time skip still blamed the refusal. Fix: result assembly replays the run-time gate over every row decided before dispatch, so each skip names the first setup that actually failed. Spec: the runtime-failure case now has a refused later setup and a refused later regular entry, and asserts that every skip names the failing setup.\n"
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-13T20:21:51.236Z","harness:claude-code","Design (2026-11-13). The setup step is a per-item linked-test field, `phase: setup` (input `phase=setup|test`; `test`, the default, is stored as absent). It is not a tests.setup settings key: a settings-declared command is executable data that would bypass the provenance and clone-trust model every linked command goes through.\n\nBehaviour:\n- Ordering. `withLinkedTestSetupEntries` (src/sdk/test/run-phases.ts) prepends every stored setup entry to a selection that names at least one regular command. `selected_indexes` lists run order (setup positions first), and `setup_indexes` lists the setups the selector did not name. A selection of setups only runs just those. test-all uses `orderLinkedTestsSetupFirst` per item.\n- Gate. `createLinkedTestSetupGate` runs inside `runLinkedTests`, which shares one sandbox, isolated root and snapshot across the item's entries, so setup output is visible to later entries. An executed setup that fails becomes `failure_category: setup_failed`, with the original class kept in `setup_failure_cause`. A refused setup keeps its category (trust_refusal or config_refusal), because its recovery differs. Either way every later entry gets a `skipped` row without executing. Setup rows carry `phase: setup` and are exempt from `--fail-on-empty-test-run` and `--require-assertions-for-pm`. They never satisfy `--measure`'s executed-test requirement (prerequisites.ts).\n- test-all. Setups are excluded from the cross-item duplicate skip. A deduplicated setup leaves an isolated or snapshot sandbox without its output (the integration spec proves this with two items).\n- Storage. `normalizeLinkedTest` keeps `phase`. `requiredItemFormatVersion` returns 3 when any test is a setup entry, and `CURRENT_ITEM_FORMAT_VERSION` is now 3, so hosts 2026.10.10 to 2026.11.13 refuse the item instead of silently dropping the field from its canonical hash. Create now stamps the required format as well (`stampCreatedItemFormat`). Before this change only edits stamped it, and create could not produce a guarded field until `phase`.\n- Identity and trust. `phase` is not in `linkedTestsHaveSameIdentity`: the same command is not both a setup and a test of one item, so convert an entry with `--set-json`. It is not in the trust fingerprint either, because the command that executes is unchanged. Merge identity hashes the whole definition, so `phase` survives three-way merges.\n- Line cap. execution.ts was at exactly 3400 effective lines. The failure-category type and counters moved to run-phases.ts and are re-exported, which leaves execution.ts at 3370.\n\nNot done (follow-up): caching a passed setup across test-all items by input digest. The issue asked for it \"where possible\", and the AC does not require it."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-13T21:36:39.624Z","harness:claude-code","When a new execution phase gates later entries, every path that can produce a row for an entry must pass through the same gate: the in-sandbox loop, pre-dispatch refusals, and any batch-level dedupe or skip. A row made outside the gate silently bypasses the dependency it declares. Batch-level caches keyed by a command (dedupe keys, effective timeouts) must also include what the command depends on (its item's setup signature). They should reserve a key only once the command really executes, or release the reservation when a prerequisite blocks it."
